 var constitutionTopics=[
 ['Powers of the central government','The constitution lists the central powers individually: foreign affairs, defense, currency, customs, citizenship, foreign trade, national transport and communications, and basic public finance. Education, culture, religion, personal status, municipal services, local development and some health and social services belong to the community administrations.','The center controls foreign affairs, defense, currency, citizenship, the national economy, customs and general taxation. Education, culture, religion and personal status belong exclusively to the communities. In municipal services, health, local roads, housing and local development, the center sets the legal framework and local administrations implement it.','General authority belongs to the central state. Only explicitly listed community matters, education, religion, culture and personal status, are reserved to community institutions. Local government exercises administrative powers delegated by the state.'],
 ['The Turkish community’s political status','The Turkish vice-presidency remains. Turks receive at least 20% representation in state institutions and elect their representatives through separate electoral rolls. General vetoes end, but laws changing Turkish constitutional status, local-government powers, language, education or security need separate Turkish approval.','The president is Greek Cypriot and the vice-president Turkish Cypriot. Absolute vetoes end. Parliament has approximately sixty Greek and fifteen Turkish members elected through separate rolls. Ordinary laws pass by general majority; changes to specifically protected Turkish rights need a reinforced majority and affirmative votes from a specified number of Turkish MPs.',null],
 ['Local government','Turkish villages and municipalities can form community groups without being geographically contiguous. A Turkish Community Assembly or Local Administration Board coordinates them. The central state retains sovereignty but cannot unilaterally alter their constitutional powers.','Every village and municipality has an elected council. Nearby or functionally linked Turkish settlements can form service unions. No separate island-wide Turkish second government is created; coordination runs through a central Local Government Ministry, with a Turkish minister as a safeguard.','Villages and municipalities are organized geographically. Turkish settlements elect their own councils, but no island-wide ethnic administrative hierarchy is established. The Interior Ministry and district administrators retain final supervision.'],
 ['Police and internal security','A single Cyprus Police remains in law. Police in Turkish settlements are effectively Turkish, and local Turkish authorities have a strong voice in daily duties and priorities. At least 20% Turkish representation and a Turkish deputy commander are constitutionally guaranteed.','A single police and gendarmerie has 20% Turkish personnel. Mixed areas use mixed units where possible; overwhelmingly Turkish areas use Turkish personnel. An independent commission controls appointments and promotion. The chief and deputy belong to different communities. Local administrations may employ civil inspectors.','The Cyprus Police is fully centralized. Approximately 20% Turkish representation is sought, but communities have no institutional control. Assigning Turkish-speaking officers to Turkish areas is administrative practice rather than a separate constitutional police power.'],
 ['Courts and constitutional disputes','One judiciary is accepted, with six Greek and three Turkish judges on the supreme court. The Turkish vice-president has a decisive role in Turkish nominations. Cases concerning Turkish constitutional rights require a panel including a Turkish judge; specialist local-government courts may be established.','The constitutional and supreme courts are merged, with six Greek and three Turkish judges. The president appoints Turkish judges from candidates preferred by the vice-president. Ordinary courts remain unified, with a right to request a judge of the party’s language or community. Local-government cases go to specialist judges within the independent judiciary.','A unified judiciary appoints judges through a common Judicial Council and gradually removes ethnic quotas. A constitutional rights catalogue and ordinary judicial review protect Turkish rights without separate courts or a Turkish veto.'],
 ['International guarantees','The 1960 Treaty of Guarantee remains, including the three guarantors and a last-resort unilateral intervention right. A permanent UN mechanism investigates complaints of violations.','The treaty remains, with a staged process: a joint constitutional commission, urgent guarantor consultations, UN verification, then joint guarantor action. A last-resort Turkish or Greek right is retained for a new Enosis coup or an immediate attack on the other community’s physical safety.','Unilateral military intervention rights end. The guarantors’ political commitments and the UN Security Council protect independence; no guarantor may use force alone.'],
 ['The prohibition of Enosis and continuity of the state','An unamendable clause prohibits Cyprus joining another state in whole or in part: Enosis, partition and annexation are forbidden. Turkey and Greece sign a joint declaration excluding Enosis and partition.','Cyprus remains an independent, sovereign, single bicommunal state. Enosis, partition and secession are prohibited. Any change requires a reinforced overall majority and separate minimum approval from Turkish and Greek MPs.',null],
 ['The position of Turkish and Greek troops','The landing force does not withdraw immediately. Withdrawal follows implementation of the constitution, Turkish local police, National Guard reforms and ratification of guarantees. Ultimately the treaty contingents remain: 650 Turkish and 950 Greek troops. TMT and Greek paramilitaries are dismantled together.','Turkish forces initially hold their ceasefire zone without expansion. Under UN supervision, the landing force and Greek officers and non-treaty personnel are reduced together. Heavy weapons are removed from the National Guard and TMT. As the new institutions function, forces return to the 650/950 treaty levels within three to six months, with future review.','All troops landed on July 20 withdraw on a short timetable. Greece withdraws coup officers and non-treaty personnel. TMT is dissolved and the National Guard returns to state control. In the longer term even the treaty contingents may be removed, with security entrusted to UN forces and international guarantees.']
 ];
 function constitutionBrief(topic){return 'Turkey has set territorial autonomy aside and proposed a revised 1960 constitutional order. The Greek delegation welcomes the opening; now both communities must agree on enforceable rules.\n\nTurkish proposal (2 Leverage Points): '+topic[1]+'\n\nCompromise (1 Leverage Point): '+topic[2]+(topic[3]?'\n\nGreek proposal (gain 0.5 Leverage Points): '+topic[3]:'\n\nA further Greek concession is unacceptable to Turkey on this issue.');}
 function beginConstitution(Q){if(Q.cyprus_campaign_resolved||Q.cyprus_atilla2_started||Q.cyprus_month!==8||Q.cyprus_day!==14||Q.cyprus_gunes_backing)return false;Q.cyprus_campaign_phase='constitutional';Q.cyprus_constitution_step=0;Q.cyprus_constitution_score=0;Q.cyprus_constitution_choices=[];Q.cyprus_roadmap_report=authoredEvents[33].body;return true;}
